blur: use a normal transform when blurring offscreen buffers

Backport of upstream scenefx PR #199, which closes upstream issue #197
("blur_ignore_transparent samples the wrong region on rotated outputs").
Applies cleanly to the 0.5 tarball. Drop this file once it is in a release.

    https://github.com/wlrfx/scenefx/pull/199
    https://github.com/wlrfx/scenefx/issues/197

WHY SYNAPSE CARRIES IT: velle's HDMI-A-1 is a 1920x1080 panel run portrait at
transform=3 (270), logical 1080x1920 — see ~/.config/synui/outputs.conf. With
backdrop blur on, the blur on that monitor came out visibly wrong (a correct
band across the top, then a hard rectangular break with the rest sampled from
the wrong part of the buffer), while DP-3 and DP-2 (both transform=0) were
perfect. Only rotated outputs are affected, which is exactly the reported
symptom: "backdrop blur distorts on the portrait monitor only".

THE BUG: get_main_buffer_blur() only ever samples and writes OFFSCREEN
framebuffers, and those are always in output-buffer orientation. It nonetheless
read tex_options.base.transform, which since upstream #154 carries the
transparency mask buffer's transform composed with the OUTPUT transform. On a
rotated output that value is 90 or 270, and it fed two separate things:

  - wlr_region_transform() on a clip region that was ALREADY in buffer
    coordinates, rotating it a second time, and
  - the set_tex_matrix() call in every downscale and upscale pass.

So the blur passes sampled and wrote the wrong part of the buffer.

Forcing the transform to normal for the whole function covers both uses, which
is why the now-redundant wlr_region_transform() call goes away rather than
being corrected. Both callers pass a COPY of the options struct, so the
transform needed afterwards to draw the stencil mask is untouched — this is the
same thing #154 already does before the final draw, just early enough that the
blur passes get it too.

diff --git a/render/fx_renderer/fx_pass.c b/render/fx_renderer/fx_pass.c
index 39b62264..b4867d30 100644
--- a/render/fx_renderer/fx_pass.c
+++ b/render/fx_renderer/fx_pass.c
@@ -994,12 +994,11 @@ static struct fx_framebuffer *get_main_buffer_blur(struct fx_gles_render_pass *p
 		return NULL;
 	}
 	fx_options->blur_data = &blur_data;
+	fx_options->tex_options.base.transform = WL_OUTPUT_TRANSFORM_NORMAL;

 	pixman_region32_t damage;
 	pixman_region32_init(&damage);
 	pixman_region32_copy(&damage, fx_options->tex_options.base.clip);
-	wlr_region_transform(&damage, &damage, fx_options->tex_options.base.transform,
-			buffer_bounds.width, buffer_bounds.height);

 	wlr_region_expand(&damage, &damage, blur_data_calc_size(&blur_data));
 	// Make sure that the region doesn't expand past the buffer bounds
